          "There is one trap here. A high average MER does not mean every extra dollar is profitable. As the budget grows from ten thousand to five hundred thousand dollars a month, the audience saturates and the Marginal ROAS falls. The extra dollar buys less than the average dollar did. So the lesson says to scale spend only up to the point of maximum aggregate dollar contribution, and to watch the break-even threshold, not the flattering average. With the money math settled, we move on to checking whether a change we made actually worked.",
          "Let us also see why Blended CAC and platform CAC can disagree. Imagine the ad platforms together claim only four out of every five new customers in the lab, because the rest bought after seeing a friend's recommendation or a post that nobody tracked. A platform-only view divides the same 75,000 dollars by a smaller count, so each customer looks more expensive than 30 dollars. The blended view divides by every new customer and shows 30 dollars. Neither number is wrong, but they answer different questions. Blended CAC tells you what the business as a whole pays to grow, and that is the figure the owner's bank balance actually feels."
